The `recover_json_object` function first identifies all content within code fences using `_FENCE.finditer(content)`. 

For the input: `fenced` will be `['{"a": 1}', '1.23']`. The code then iterates through `reversed(pool)` (where `pool` is `fenced`). 
1. It checks `'1.23'`. `loads_strict` (or `_loads_last_wins`) will fail to return a `dict` because a float is not a dictionary. 
2. It checks `'{"a": 1}'`. This parses as a `dict`, so it is returned.

For the rewritten text: `fenced` will be `['1.23', '{"a": 1}']`. The code iterates through `reversed(pool)`:
1. It checks `'{"a": 1}'`. This parses as a `dict`, so it is returned immediately.

In both cases, the recovered object is `{"a": 1}`. Since the recovered object is the same, the grounding kernel's answer (which depends on the fields of the recovered object) will be unchanged.